 "_note": "Grade 1 long vowels (English literacy year, Part 12.3; types/_shared/vowels-g1.js). General American pronunciation. `pics`: the library picture of a word (only pictures a first grader names with that word; looked at on a contact sheet: the boat is the small toys/boat, never the ship of beach/boat; tub is the bathtub, rod the fishing rod, plane the airplane, cane the candy cane, bike the bicycle, plate the white plate, globe the classroom globe). `silentE`: `pairs` by vowel (a short-vowel word and the word it becomes with a silent e, both real words a first grader knows; hug/huge is left out because the g changes too), `pictured` the words of those pairs that have a picture (the picture shows that word; cape is left out: its picture is a buttoned coat), `write` silent-e words with a picture that a child writes under it on Level 3 (none for u_e), `sentences` a sentence with a blank that only ONE word of a pair fits. `noWrite`: words a child is never asked to write under a picture, because the picture can be named another way (jeep: car; seal: sea lion; rose: flower; cube: box or block; dice: die; cape: coat; rope, cone, hose: the pictures read as other things; peach: the library peaches look like apples, so no peach is in this bank). `teams`: for each pair of spellings of one long vowel, `a` and `b` (the letters `g` and one-syllable words in which they spell the long vowel; read is left out because it has two pronunciations), `rule` where the place in the word tells the spelling (null where it does not; the words of its examples are never printed again on a page that shows the rule), `odd` a word that has one of the two spellings but does NOT say the long vowel (the word to cross out on Level 3), `write` the pictured words a child writes under their pictures on Level 3 (the first letter is printed at the start of the row; that is why rain, whose picture is a rain cloud, and toast, which a child might call bread, may stand here), `box` words that fit NONE of the sentences (they fill the word box), `sentences` with a blank and the one word that fits, and `apart` pairs of sentence answers that never stand in one word box (each would make the other sentence half true: \"Wash your hands with snow.\").",
